{"id": "cma-95208", "status": "ok", "meta": "A seasonal landscape from a set of four hanging scrolls by Ren Yu, the least prolific of the Four Rens, 1892.", "about": "This seasonal landscape belongs to a set of four hanging scrolls by Ren Yu, the youngest, most eccentric and least prolific of the Four Rens, a family of prominent painters in Shanghai in the late Qing period. Perhaps because of his opium habit and the money troubles that followed, he tended to be lackadaisical in his work. The few good paintings left hint at the promise lost to opium. Charles Lang Freer acquired his paintings and gave them to the museum.", "museum_record": "https://clevelandart.org/art/1915.97.3", "inventory_number": "1915.97.3", "dimensions": "", "credit_line": "Gift of Charles L. Freer", "sources": ["https://clevelandart.org/art/1915.97.3", "https://openaccess-api.clevelandart.org/api/artworks/95208"], "check": "clean"}
